  </nav>

  <section id="brain-tour" aria-labelledby="brain-tour-title">
    <div class="section-copy">
      <h2 id="brain-tour-title">The admin interface keeps the archive usable.</h2>
      <p>This walkthrough shows the administrative side of Second Brain: ingest queues, source review, wiki pages, duplicate checks, and conflict review. A team member asking a question never sees this interface. They simply talk to Second Brain in a familiar chat.</p>
      <p class="recording-note">I’m sorry that parts of the interface in this recording are in Russian. This may make it harder to follow.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="product-video">
      <video
        controls
        muted
        playsinline
        preload="metadata"
        poster="/media/shum-brain/shum-brain-walkthrough-poster.webp"
        width="1600"
        height="900"
      >
        <source src="/media/shum-brain/shum-brain-walkthrough.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
        Your browser does not support HTML video.
      </video>
      <figcaption>The walkthrough shows the tools behind the chat experience.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="brain-ingest" aria-labelledby="brain-ingest-title">
    <div class="section-copy">
      <h2 id="brain-ingest-title">Ingest turns mixed media into searchable evidence.</h2>
      <p>SHUM projects do not live in clean text documents. The system extracts useful content from documents, images, audio, and video, then keeps the original file context on every fragment.</p>
    </div>
    <figure>
      <div class="ingest-pipeline">
        <div class="ingest-sources">
          <h3>Project material</h3>
          <div><span>Documents</span><span>Images</span><span>Audio</span><span>Video</span><span>Notes and chats</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="pipeline-connector"><span>extracts</span><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></div>
        <div class="extractors">
          <h3>Readable evidence</h3>
          <div><span>Text and document structure</span><span>Image descriptions</span><span>Transcripts and video summaries</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="pipeline-connector"><span>normalizes</span><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></div>
        <div class="chunk-record">
          <h3>Source fragment</h3>
          <dl><div><dt>Content</dt><dd>Extracted text</dd></div><div><dt>Context</dt><dd>Project + file</dd></div><div><dt>Metadata</dt><dd>Date + media type</dd></div></dl>
        </div>
        <div class="pipeline-connector"><span>indexes</span><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></div>
        <div class="search-indexes">
          <h3>Two search indexes</h3>
          <div><span><strong>ChromaDB</strong>Meaning</span><span><strong>SQLite FTS5</strong>Exact terms</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>Ingest converts each source into searchable fragments while preserving the project, file, date, and media type needed to verify an answer.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="brain-search" aria-labelledby="brain-search-title">
    <div class="section-copy">
      <h2 id="brain-search-title">RAG finds projects even with different words.</h2>
      <p>A team member may remember an effect, a season, or one production detail instead of a filename. Hybrid retrieval combines meaning, exact terms, and file dates before an AI agent writes the answer.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="search-figure" data-brain-search data-current="4">
      <div class="query-node" data-search-part="1">
        <span class="question-label">The same example question</span>
        <p>“What project did we make last May that used liquid-metal graphics?”</p>
      </div>
      <div class="search-lanes" data-search-part="2">
        <article>
          <h3>Match meaning</h3>
          <p>Find related descriptions even when the wording changes.</p>
          <span class="lane-term">small space ↔ short throw</span>
          <span class="engine-label">ChromaDB</span>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Match exact terms</h3>
          <p>Keep names and technical terms searchable as written.</p>
          <span class="lane-term">lens, projector, model name</span>
          <span class="engine-label">SQLite FTS5 / BM25</span>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Consider recency</h3>
          <p>Bring recently modified material into the candidate set.</p>
          <span class="lane-term">file modification date</span>
          <span class="engine-label">Temporal retrieval</span>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="merge-bridge" data-search-part="3"><span>combines the matches</span><b aria-hidden="true">↓</b><strong>One evidence set, with source IDs</strong><span>Meaning, exact terms, and file dates all contribute</span></div>
      <div class="evidence-result" data-search-part="4">
        <div>
          <h3>Give the agent RAG context</h3>
          <p>Source text, file context, and a confidence state travel together.</p>
        </div>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Fragment</dt><dd><code>chunk_id</code> + source text</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Provenance</dt><dd>File, project scope, modification date</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Confidence</dt><dd>High / medium / low / conflict</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </div>
      <div class="search-controls" hidden>
        <div class="step-buttons" role="group" aria-label="Explore the search process">
          <button type="button" data-search-step="1" aria-pressed="false">Ask</button>
          <button type="button" data-search-step="2" aria-pressed="false">Search</button>
          <button type="button" data-search-step="3" aria-pressed="false">Combine</button>
          <button type="button" data-search-step="4" aria-pressed="true">Give context</button>
        </div>
        <p class="step-caption" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true">The caller receives source fragments and confidence, then uses them to compose an answer.</p>
      </div>
      <figcaption>RAG retrieves the evidence first. The AI agent then writes an answer that points back to the original project material.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="brain-wiki" aria-labelledby="brain-wiki-title">
    <div class="section-copy">
      <h2 id="brain-wiki-title">A summary should not overwrite human judgement.</h2>
      <p>The wiki keeps generated summaries and human notes in separate blocks. Updating the AI block preserves the human block and saves the previous version.</p>
    </div>
    <figure>
      <div class="wiki-mechanism">
        <div class="wiki-input">
          <h3>Source fragments</h3>
          <div class="fragment-sheet"><span>Extracted text</span><span>Source identifier</span><span>File context</span></div>
          <div class="vertical-connector"><span>grounds a summary</span><b aria-hidden="true">↓</b></div>
          <p>Citations connect generated claims to the material behind them.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="wiki-page">
          <h3>Project wiki</h3>
          <div class="wiki-block generated"><strong>AI summary</strong><span>Regenerated from source fragments</span><span class="citation-line">Claim <span class="citation-ref">[source]</span></span></div>
          <div class="wiki-block protected"><strong>Human notes</strong><span>Edited by a person</span><span class="protected-line"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M7 10V7a5 5 0 0 1 10 0v3M5 10h14v12H5z" /></svg>Preserved during AI updates</span></div>
          <div class="wiki-history"><span aria-hidden="true">↳</span> Previous AI version saved to history</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>The update boundary is a block within the page. Human notes survive regeneration; the AI summary keeps source references.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="under-figure">The structure works, but source extraction and summary quality still need improvement. A valid citation alone does not prove the model interpreted the source correctly.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="brain-graph" aria-labelledby="brain-graph-title">
    <div class="section-copy">
      <h2 id="brain-graph-title">Remember Obsidian graphs? I built one we could use.</h2>
      <p>Second Brain’s 3D graph makes project scopes, files, fragments, media, and people navigable. The interface uses <a href="https://github.com/vasturiano/react-force-graph" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener" aria-label="react-force-graph-3d on GitHub (opens in a new tab)">react-force-graph-3d</a>, the React wrapper built on <a href="https://github.com/vasturiano/3d-force-graph" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener" aria-label="3d-force-graph on GitHub (opens in a new tab)">3d-force-graph</a>.</p>
      <p class="recording-note">I’m sorry that parts of the interface in this recording are in Russian. This may make it harder to follow.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="product-video">
      <video
        controls
        muted
        playsinline
        preload="metadata"
        poster="/media/shum-brain/shum-brain-graph-poster.webp"
        width="1600"
        height="900"
